2013 में वैज्ञानिक नॉर्बर्ट युर्गेन्स ने बताया कि रेत की एक दीमक, *Psammotermes allocerus*, उनके अध्ययन किए लगभग हर परी-घेरे में मिली। ये दीमकें रेत में रहती हैं और घास की जड़ें खाती हैं। यानी ये घास पर पलने वाली, [[herbivore|शाकाहारी]] कीड़े हैं।

उनका विचार है कि खाली टुकड़ा दीमकें बनाती हैं। घास न हो, तो बारिश का पानी रेत में समा जाता है और पौधे उसे खींच नहीं पाते। घेरा ज़मीन के नीचे पानी का एक छिपा भंडार बन जाता है। लंबे [[@droughts|सूखों]] में दीमकें वहीं बची रहती हैं। उन्होंने इनकी तुलना [[ecosystem|पारिस्थितिकी तंत्र]] के इंजीनियरों से की, जैसे बाँध बनाने वाले ऊदबिलाव।

{{story:termite-mounds}}

The [[@milky-way|Milky Way]] has been growing by eating smaller galaxies for billions of years. Data from Europe's Gaia space telescope revealed stars left over from a galaxy, nicknamed Gaia-Enceladus, that merged with ours roughly 10 billion years ago. Its stars move differently from others, like guests who arrived late to a party. Astronomers think big galaxies like ours were built up this way, swallowing many smaller ones over billions of years.

Right now, the Milky Way is pulling apart the Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy, stretching it into a long stream of stars wrapped around us. The Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy visible from the southern half of the Earth, may crash into the Milky Way in a couple of billion years.

{{story:probability}}

Mantas have clever ways of eating. Sometimes they swim in tight backward loops, turning somersault after somersault through a thick patch of plankton. Sometimes many mantas line up nose to tail and swim in a circle, forming a feeding chain that may concentrate the food.

They often feed where currents push plankton together. In Hanifaru Bay in the [[@maldives|Maldives]], dozens or even more than a hundred mantas can gather to feed at once. In Kona, Hawaii, dive boats shine bright lights into the water at night. The lights attract plankton, and mantas glide in to feed, doing slow somersaults just in front of the divers. Mantas have long been part of [[@hawaii-culture|Hawaii's]] ocean life and stories.

{{story:fingerprints}}

People first met magnets as a dark rock called lodestone, a natural magnet made of an iron mineral. The ancient Greeks found it near a place called Magnesia, which may be where the word "magnet" comes from.

In China, people discovered that a sliver of lodestone, free to turn, always points north and south. By about 900 years ago, Chinese sailors were using compasses to navigate. The idea spread to Arab, Indian and European sailors, and changed how people made [[@maps-history|maps]] and crossed oceans.

{{story:earth-magnet}}

सबसे अनोखी किस्मों में से एक है रागमाला, यानी "रागों की माला"। भारतीय शास्त्रीय संगीत में [[@raga-time|राग]] सुरों का एक समूह है जिसका अपना मिज़ाज होता है, और बहुत से राग दिन के किसी समय या किसी मौसम के होते हैं। चित्रकारों ने हर [[raga|राग]] को एक तस्वीर दे दी।

मानसून के किसी राग में काले बादलों के नीचे प्रेमी दिख सकते हैं, साथ में नाचते मोर और आसमान चीरती बिजली। सुबह के किसी कोमल राग में कोई स्त्री पक्षियों को दाना खिलाती दिख सकती है। पूरी रागमाला में 36 या उससे भी ज़्यादा चित्र हो सकते थे। इस तरह संगीत हाथ में थामने लायक चीज़ बन गया, और चित्र लगभग सुनाई देने लगा।

{{story:raga-time}}

Elephants are eating machines. An adult can munch through around 150 kg of grass, leaves and branches a day, about the weight of two grown men. Much of a mahout's morning goes on cutting fodder or leading the elephant to graze.

Then comes bath time. Mahouts walk their elephants into a river or pond, where the animal lies on its side while the mahout scrubs its thick skin with a coconut husk or a stone. Elephants have no sweat [[gland|glands]] spread over their bodies the way we do, so water and mud help keep them cool.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

The word "manga" is old. The artist Katsushika Hokusai, famous for his woodblock print of a [[@waves-how|giant wave]], used it in 1814 for books of his lively sketches. Modern manga took off after the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]].

The most important early artist was Osamu Tezuka, often called "the god of manga". He loved American cartoons and borrowed their large eyes, then added the fast pace and close-ups of films. His robot boy *Astro Boy* became a manga in 1952 and a weekly TV cartoon in 1963. To save money, his studio reused drawings and moved only mouths and eyes, a style still seen in anime today. You can compare it with [[@animation-how|how cartoons are made]].

{{story:japan-culture}}

20वीं सदी के ज़्यादातर हिस्से में बहुत-सी स्कूली किताबें "जीभ का नक्शा" दिखाती थीं। उसमें मीठा आगे, कड़वा पीछे, और खट्टा-नमकीन किनारों पर होता था। यह ग़लत है। 1901 में एक जर्मन वैज्ञानिक ने पाया था कि जीभ के कुछ हिस्से कुछ स्वादों के लिए बहुत ही थोड़े ज़्यादा संवेदनशील हैं। बाद के लेखकों ने इसे एक सख़्त नक्शा बना दिया।

जीभ का हर वह हिस्सा जिसमें स्वाद कलिकाएँ हैं, पाँचों स्वाद पहचान सकता है। स्वाद कलिकाएँ तालू और गले में भी उगती हैं। कैटफ़िश, यानी मांगुर मछली, तो इससे भी आगे है: उसकी पूरी त्वचा पर स्वाद कलिकाएँ होती हैं, इसलिए वह अपने पूरे शरीर के आस-पास के पानी को चख सकती है।

{{story:spicy-hot}}

Kay Zufall ran a nursery school in New Jersey and was the sister-in-law of Joe McVicker, who worked at Kutol. She read that wallpaper cleaner could be used for modelling, bought some and tried it with her class. The children loved it.

She told McVicker to remove the cleaning chemicals, add colour and sell it as a [[@toys-history|toy]]. The company wanted to call it "Rainbow Modeling Compound". Zufall thought of something shorter: Play-Doh.

{{story:toys-history}}

Crickets and mealworms are packed with protein. They need far less feed and water than cattle, and they can be farmed in stacked boxes. In Thailand, fried insects are a street snack. In parts of Odisha, Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand, red ant chutney called *chaprah* or *kai* has been eaten for generations.

Many people in the US and India still find the idea strange. So some companies grind [[@insects-food|insects]] into flour and hide them in pasta, crisps and protein bars.

{{story:space-food}}

In 1939, Bhabha came to India for a holiday. Then the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]] broke out, and he could not return to Europe. He took a job at the Indian Institute of Science in Bangalore, working in the department led by the Nobel winner C. V. Raman.

It turned out to be a lucky accident. Bhabha decided India needed its own centre for advanced physics. In 1944 he wrote to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust asking for support. The next year the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research opened, first in borrowed rooms at the institute in Bangalore and then, from late 1945, in his aunt's bungalow in Bombay.

{{story:nuclear-power}}

In South Africa, Indians faced harsh discrimination. On a train to Pretoria in 1893, Gandhi refused to leave a first-class carriage for a white passenger and was thrown off at Pietermaritzburg station.

He spent the night shivering in the waiting room and decided to fight the injustice. He stayed 21 years. There he developed satyagraha, leading Indians to burn their identity passes and court arrest. He went to jail several times, but won some changes in the law.

{{story:khadi}}

The train set off at 3:35 pm. Its guests included British officials, wealthy merchants and some of Bombay's leading citizens. Along the way, people gathered at every village to stare at the hissing, smoking engines.

Some onlookers had heard frightening rumours about the "fire carriage". Others simply came to see something new. The engines, built in England and shipped to Bombay, were named Sahib, Sindh and Sultan. The train stopped at Sion to take on water and reached Thane just before 5 pm. Fares for ordinary passengers were soon set in three classes. Ordinary travellers soon packed the cheapest carriages, and the line quickly proved that Indians were keen to ride trains.

{{story:steam-engine}}

Almost all the moai came from one quarry, Rano Raraku, an old [[@volcano|volcano]] [[crater|crater]] made of soft rock called tuff. Carvers used hard [[@stone-tools|stone tools]], called toki, to shape each figure while it was still attached to the hillside. Then they cut it free and slid it down the slope.

An average moai is about 4 m tall and weighs about as much as two elephants. The largest one ever finished stands about 10 m tall. One unfinished giant, still lying in the quarry, would have been about 21 m, as tall as a seven-storey building. Some moai were later given red stone topknots, called pukao, made from a different volcanic rock.

{{story:sculpture}}

Long ago, the Rann was an arm of the Arabian Sea, deep enough for boats. Over thousands of years, rivers carried in mud and the land slowly rose, partly because of [[@earthquakes|earthquakes]] along faults under Kutch. The sea retreated, leaving a huge flat basin barely above sea level.

The ground still moves. In 1819, a powerful earthquake pushed up a long ridge of land that locals named Allah Bund, meaning "dam of God". In 2001, the Bhuj earthquake badly damaged towns across Kutch. People rebuilt, and many towns are larger now than before.

{{story:flamingos-pink}}

In 1154, the scholar al-Idrisi finished a world map for King Roger II of Sicily. He gathered reports from sailors and traders for about 15 years. His map put south at the top, which was normal for many mapmakers in the Islamic world.

Which way is "up" on a map is a choice, not a rule. North went on top in Europe much later, partly because of the compass.

{{story:gps-how}}

In Japanese [[folklore|folklore]], a kitsune is a fox with magic powers, especially shape-shifting. The older and wiser a fox becomes, the more tails it grows, up to nine. Some kitsune are [[trickster|tricksters]]. Others are messengers of Inari, the spirit of rice and farming.

At Fushimi Inari Shrine in [[@kyoto|Kyoto]], stone foxes guard paths lined with thousands of bright orange gates, stretching up a mountain. If you know the Pokémon Ninetales, you've already met a kitsune.

{{story:studio-ghibli}}

Riddles work by describing an ordinary thing in an unusual way. A riddle might call an egg "a little white house with a yellow treasure and no door". Your brain has to let go of the obvious meaning and find a new one.

Some riddles use [[metaphor|metaphor]], describing one thing as another. Some use [[@words-puns|puns]], where a word has two meanings. "What has keys but can't open locks?" A piano. The answer seems obvious once you know it, which is part of the fun. In [[@puzzles-logic|logic puzzles]], you reason step by step. In a riddle, you often need a sideways leap.

{{story:brain-works}}

In one tale, rats are eating the city's grain. The king gives every household a cat and a cow, so the cat can drink milk and grow strong. Tenali's cat grows thin while everyone else's grows fat.

The truth is sneakier. On the first day, Tenali gave his cat a bowl of milk so hot that it burnt its mouth. After that the cat ran from milk. When the king comes to inspect, Tenali sets down a bowl, and the cat flees. The king learns that a gift with silly rules can backfire. Tenali keeps the cow.

{{story:hampi}}
